URL Shortener
=============
This project takes a long URL and returns a short code. Visit the code on the internet, get redirected to the long URL.
Stack: Python, Flask, gunicorn, PostgreSQL 17, Redis 7, nginx, Docker Compose.
See Architecture diagram in architecture.png


To run it
---------
cp .env.example .env # then set your own values
docker compose up -d --build --scale app=3
docker compose exec -T postgres sh -c 'psql -U "$POSTGRES_USER" -d "$POSTGRES_DB"' < schema.sql

UI at http://127.0.0.1:8080.


v1: Error's and collisions Handling
------------------------------------
Setup: This setup consist of two endpoints and a Python dictionary.

Working: You send it a long URL, it gives you a short code. You visit that code, it sends you to the long URL.

* Problem 1a: Requests without the url field will return a 500. 
       Meaning our code broke, meanwhile what has actually happened is that the user has sent a bad request and they should get a 400 which means the client has sent a malformed request.
------------------------------------------------
| # e.g. request says "link" instead of "url"  |
| curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:5001/shorten \ |
| -H "Content-Type: application/json" \        |
| -d '{"link": "https://example.com"}'         |
------------------------------------------------
* Fix 1a: Logic added to reject a request if the url field is missing

* Problem 1b: Two links can end up with the same code.
       The token_urlsafe(4) function in the v1 code can make 4.29 billion different short codes. That sounds like plenty. But once 10 million codes are in use, each new code has a 0.23% chance of the code already being taken. That is about 23,000 collisions. Collison here means two different links ending up with the same code.
* Fix 1b: When a code is already taken, make a new one and try again.

* Problem 1c: If a user visits a code that does not exist in our DB, we return a 500. 
       We should instead return a 404 which is a *Not found* error code, meaning "We looked, it is not here". 
* Fix 1c: We add a check

Fixes are implemented in v2-app.py


v2: Storing the short codes in memory
--------------------------------------
Setup: This setup consist of same two endpoints and a Python dictionary. but this time with fixes to issues pointed out in v1

* Problem with v2: Codes still live in the app memory, if there is an app restart that data will be lost
* Fix: Add a DB, SQLite

Fix is implemented in v3-app.py


v3: Making it survive a restart
-------------------------------
Setup: In v2 the dictionary died on every restart , we fixed this in v3 by introducing SQLite, which keeps the links in a file on disk, so a restart no longer wipes them.

Database Design: The code is the primary key, because every that's what a user types in and their expected result is a long url so the DB is built around that.

* Problem: SQLite only works on one machine. 
       It stops two writers clashing by locking the file through the operating system, and that only works while both are on the same machine asking the same file. What if the writers are on different machines?
* Fix: We swap SQLite for postgres 
       With SQLite, every copy of your app opens links.db itself. Two copies on two machines open the same file and both write to it.
       With Postgres, your app never opens a file. It sends the SQL over the network and Postgres does the writing. However many copies you run, only one program ever touches the data, so nothing clashes.

Fix is implemented in v4-app.py


v4: Running on more than one machine
-------------------------------------
Setup: Postgres replaces SQLite, with a connection pool and Redis in front.

* Problem 4a: Postgres was slower. 
       0.22ms to 10.9ms for the same query, because psycopg.connect() sat inside the function and every request opened a brand new connection.
* Fix 4a: A connection pool. 
       Connections open once at startup and get lent out. 825 to 3759 req/sec, for moving one line.

* Problem 4b: The database was still half the request.
* Fix 4b: Check Redis first. 
       If the code is there, Postgres is never touched. 3759 to 5386 req/sec.


Running more than one copy
---------------------------
No app code changed, so there is no v5 file.

* Problem: All three containers wanted port 5001, and only one program can hold a port.
* Fix: Stop publishing the app's port. Put nginx on 8080 and let it pick a copy for each request. 12 requests spread 3 / 3 / 6.

Nothing broke, because the data lives in Postgres and all three copies see the same links.